  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA lower stratus cloud deck will be moving in during the early \nmorning and will stick around through the day. These low clouds will \ncause some lower visibilities to develop over portions of north \ncentral and northeastern SD during the morning, which could be less \nthan 1 mile at time. The clouds will rise during the morning into \nthe afternoon, so there will not be lower visibilities during the \nafternoon into the evening. Higher pressure will be moving in during \nthe day and stick around through Friday, which will help to keep \nprecipitation chances out. The upper and mid level flow from the \nnorthwest/north will help to push colder air in. This cold air will \nstart to cool temperatures today and through the rest of the week. \nThe coldest temperatures will happen on Thursday, with highs in the \n30s to low 40s and lows in the 20s to teens into Friday morning. \n\nA surface low will be developing over the Rockies Thursday into \nFriday. With this low to the west and high pressure over \nnortheastern SD, the pressure gradient over western and central SD \nwill start to tighten. As the high pressure moves east Saturday into \nSunday, the tight pressure gradient will spread over northeastern SD \nas well. This tight pressure gradient will help to strengthen the \nwind Friday through Sunday. The strongest wind gusts will be west of \nthe Missouri River, with a 30-50% chance for gusts up to or greater \nthan 35 mph on Friday and 50-80% chance on Saturday. An eye might \nneed to be kept on these winds in the upcoming model runs to see if \nthey might increase in strength or if humidity decreases and fire \nweather concerns increase. The models have been shifting the \ntimeline of when the warmer air will be pushing back into SD later \nin the previous few runs. Currently, the models are forecasting a \nweak warm front moving through Sunday that will push the warm air \nback in and warm temperatures above normal.",
